WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.880
Though SQL is a keyword-based language, it
is a programming language.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:11.550
With any programming language, what we want
to happen and what happens often differ.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:16.490
In this domain on troubleshooting, we will
see how to troubleshoot both syntax and runtime

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:17.490
errors.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:22.739
As we do this, take note of the most common
mistakes people make when building queries.

00:00:22.739 --> 00:00:27.320
This way, if this happens to you, you will
know what to do.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:34.370
This domain is all about going through two
types of errors: syntax errors and runtime

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:35.500
errors.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:41.110
For the most part, a syntax error will cause
a statement not to run, and a runtime error

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:46.260
will appear when there are no syntax errors
present, but something in a query is causing

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.360
it not to run properly.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.080
Some errors occur when trying to work with
database objects.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:59.880
Take a look at this statement attempting to
add a column to an existing table in the BikeRepair

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.020
database.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:09.640
I will execute this statement, and immediately
we see an error about an object type.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:14.570
This error is a syntax error because whoever
built this query did not specify the type

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:17.040
of object being altered.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:24.659
I will change this to an ALTER TABLE statement.

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:27.770
I will attempt to execute this again.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:34.710
Now, it is saying there is incorrect syntax
near the keyword column.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:41.040
When adding something to a table, SQL knows
we are adding a column, so the keyword is

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.490
unnecessary.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.259
Let's try this again.

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:50.029
Now, when we execute it, this works.

00:01:50.029 --> 00:01:56.240
Let me show you a runtime error as it pertains
to working with objects.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:03.789
I will run this statement, free of syntax
errors, and we get an error stating that every

00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:06.529
column in a table must be unique.

00:02:06.529 --> 00:02:13.250
Again, a runtime error is an error that is
not caused by a badly written query.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:18.250
A runtime error occurs when a well-written
query attempts to do something to a database

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:21.200
that cannot be done.

